{"abstract":"Public encounters are an essential element in citizen–state interactions. Yet, we know very little about the interactional dynamics between clients and street-level bureaucrats. By analyzing data from interviews and participatory observations of public encounters in a social security administration context, we propose a typology of public encounters based on clients' and employees' preparedness that affects the dynamics and outcomes of services. Encounters can either be characterized by “Conflict and Obstruction,” “Advocacy,” “Case Processing,” or “Agreement and Collaboration.” We conceptualize the relation between these types and how both clients and caseworkers transition between them. Additionally, the article's findings suggest that public encounters should not be observed as singular events. Rather, spillover effects between encounters and long-term multi-episode interactions with clients prove to be essential in understanding behavior on both sides through mutual learning.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Döring, Matthias","Drathschmidt, Nicolas","Nielsen, Stine Piilgaard Porner"],"cuid":["S02-095.83-803699"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Döring, Matthias"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Potsdam"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"it takes at least two to tango investigating interactional dynamics between clients and caseworkers in public encounters","src":["aau","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/puar.13816","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-095.83-803699","identifier":[{"id":"85190474013","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-5fdab2bf-c169-4b04-a54e-8ae63b095f16","sdu-c1fb9196-7a22-49e5-b95e-3eba22f384e2"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-2C2709EC61178F0DEEEB467EFC83AA1F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/712424537/Public_Administration_Review_-_2024_-_D_ring_-_It_takes_at_least_two_to_tango_Investigating_interactional_dynamics.pdf","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/287416756/Public_Administration_Review_-_2024_-_D_ring_-_It_takes_at_least_two_to_tango_Investigating_interactional_dynamics.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/PUAR.13816","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Business and Social Sciences, Department of Political Science and Public Management","SDU, The Faculty of Business and Social Sciences, Department of Political Science and Public Management, Public Administration, Welfare and Politics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"university of potsdam","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Potsdam","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Law","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Law, Den socialretlige forskningsgruppe","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Law, Forskningsgruppen - Offentligret"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Matthias","key":"döringpau","last":"Döring","locid":"0ee75271-e7d1-48d4-ae2e-a7e45882ff93","name":"Döring, Matthias","orcid":"0000-0002-5510-2579","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Nicolas","key":"drathschmidtpau","last":"Drathschmidt","name":"Drathschmidt, Nicolas","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Stine Piilgaard Porner","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"15d13939-41d9-42d1-b011-51b35353962e","name":"Nielsen, Stine Piilgaard Porner","orcid":"0000-0002-2889-5104","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/puar.13816","issn":["00333352","15406210"],"issn_org":["00333352"],"issue":"2","journal":"Public Administration Review","pages":"419-435","publisher":"Wiley","status":"p","title":"Public Administration Review","volume":"85"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85190474013"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["E-Published (ahead of print)","Published"]},"sid":["aau-5fdab2bf-c169-4b04-a54e-8ae63b095f16","sdu-c1fb9196-7a22-49e5-b95e-3eba22f384e2"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"It Takes (at least) Two to Tango - Investigating Interactional Dynamics between Clients and Caseworkers in Public Encounters","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-095.83-803699","year":2025,"yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"PLATO (PLAnetary Transits and Oscillations of stars) is ESA’s M3 mission designed to detect and characterise extrasolar planets and perform asteroseismic monitoring of a large number of stars. PLATO will detect small planets (down to <2REarth) around bright stars (<11 mag), including terrestrial planets in the habitable zone of solar-like stars. With the complement of radial velocity observations from the ground, planets will be characterised for their radius, mass, and age with high accuracy (5%, 10%, 10% for an Earth-Sun combination respectively). PLATO will provide us with a large-scale catalogue of well-characterised small planets up to intermediate orbital periods, relevant for a meaningful comparison to planet formation theories and to better understand planet evolution. It will make possible comparative exoplanetology to place our Solar System planets in a broader context. In parallel, PLATO will study (host) stars using asteroseismology, allowing us to determine the stellar properties with high accuracy, substantially enhancing our knowledge of stellar structure and evolution. The payload instrument consists of 26 cameras with 12cm aperture each. For at least four years, the mission will perform high-precision photometric measurements. Here we review the science objectives, present PLATO‘s target samples and fields, provide an overview of expected core science performance as well as a description of the instrument and the mission profile towards the end of the serial production of the flight cameras. PLATO is scheduled for a launch date end 2026. This overview therefore provides a summary of the mission to the community in preparation of the upcoming operational phases.","author_count":849,"contributors":["Rauer, Heike","Aerts, Conny","Cabrera, Juan","Deleuil, Magali","Erikson, Anders","Gizon, Laurent","Goupil, Mariejo","Heras, Ana","Walloschek, Thomas","Lorenzo-Alvarez, Jose","Marliani, Filippo","Martin-Garcia, César","Mas-Hesse, J. Miguel","O’