{"abstract":"Background and ObjectivesThe incidence of CNS tumors has increased over the recent decades, and few risk factors are identified. Ultrafine particles (UFPs) can cross the blood-brain barrier and thereby cause direct intracranial exposure. The aim of this cohort study was to study the possible relationship between air pollution exposure including UFPs and tumors of the CNS.MethodsThe study base included all Danish adults (aged 20 years and older) identified from nationwide registries since 1991. We defined the end point as the first, primary CNS tumor identified in the Danish Cancer Registry. We applied Danish national registers for address histories and covariates and a state-of-the-art, validated model for assessment of residential air pollution. Cox proportional hazards models were used to estimate hazard ratios (HRs) with 95% CIs for tumors of the cranial nerves, meningioma, and glioma associated with air pollution exposure. We conducted analyses based on a national emission inventory to allocate air pollution concentrations to contributions from local traffic and other sources. Covariates included socioeconomic and demographic factors at both individual and area levels.ResultsThe study included 3,959,619 adults (mean age 35 years, 49.6% female) and 16,596 cases of CNS tumors. Ten-year mean exposure to UFPs, fine particulate matter (PM2.5), nitrogen dioxide (NO2), and elemental carbon (EC) was associated with the risk of developing meningioma with confounder-adjusted HRs (95% CI) of 1.10 (1.05-1.16) per interquartile range (IQR) for UFPs; 1.21 (1.10, 1.34) per IQR for PM2.5; 1.12 (1.07, 1.18) per IQR for NO2; and 1.03 (1.00, 1.05) per IQR for EC. Source-specific analyses indicated that air pollution from both local traffic and other sources could be influential. Corresponding HRs observed for the other CNS groups were as follows: tumors of the cranial nerves (n = 2,342): 0.94 (0.86-1.02), 0.89 (0.76-1.03), 0.90 (0.83-0.97), and 0.92 (0.87-0.98); glioma (n = 6,197): 1.01 (0.96-1.06), 0.95 (0.87-1.04), 1.02 (0.97-1.06), and 1.01 (0.99-1.04); and other CNS tumors (n = 3,412): 0.96 (0.90-1.02), 0.89 (0.80-1.00), 0.99 (0.94-1.05), and 1.01 (0.98, 1.05).DiscussionThe findings of this nationwide register-based cohort study indicated that air pollution might contribute to the development of meningioma.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Hvidtfeldt, Ulla A.","Sørensen, Mette","Poulsen, Aslak H.","Ketzel, Matthias","Brandt, Jørgen","Thygesen, Lau C.","Wohlfahrt, Jan","Raaschou-Nielsen, Ole"],"cuid":["S03-099.71-822543"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"corresponding_author":["Hvidtfeldt, Ulla A."],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-NP","DI-U"],"level3":{"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-CAN"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-RUC","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-NP-CAN","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-RUC","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"ambient outdoor air pollution and risk of tumors of the central nervous system","src":["au","ruc","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-RUC","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105010409796","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S03-099.71-822543","identifier":[{"id":"105010409796","type":"scopus"},{"id":"40632980","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-132dcd8c-64cf-4296-9466-6607ce2c1bb8","ruc-16356bb6-9739-4be9-b969-c156e3245da2","sdu-2f5ea317-f8a1-43f0-8556-882590a4bb15"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-D889572393AB3F5B87E4C56501D9D360"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"dcrc danish cancer society research center","l2":"DI-NP","l2n":"Non-Profit Organisations","l3":"DI-NP-CAN","l3nn":"DCRC Danish Cancer Society Research Center","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DCRC Danish Cancer Society Research Center","namevar":["Danish Cancer Society"],"nora_code":"DI-NP-CAN","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ruc roskilde university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-RUC","l3nl":"Roskilde University","l3nn":"RUC Roskilde University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"RUC Roskilde University","namevar":["Roskilde University, Department of Science and Environment","Roskilde University, Department of Science and Environment, Molecular and Medical Biology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-RUC","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, Department of Environmental Science, Department of Environmental Science - Atmospheric Emissions & Modelling"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, National Institute of Public Health","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, National Institute of Public Health, Public health epidemiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"dk","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University (AAU)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ulla A.","key":"hvidtfeldtpau","last":"Hvidtfeldt","name":"Hvidtfeldt, Ulla A.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","country":"dk","first":"Mette","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"5bf2a665-def4-499c-9c63-0c440da72e20","name":"Sørensen, Mette","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Aslak H.","key":"poulsenpau","last":"Poulsen","name":"Poulsen, Aslak H.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Matthias","key":"ketzelpau","last":"Ketzel","locid":"798900d2-1d54-4c53-a4ca-671c12aa3015","name":"Ketzel, Matthias","orcid":"0000-0001-9519-1935","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jørgen","key":"brandtpau","last":"Brandt","locid":"5f570c62-a5a5-4760-b7c7-8755b6e37ebf","name":"Brandt, Jørgen","orcid":"0000-0002-7580-9547","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Lau C.","key":"thygesenpau","last":"Thygesen","locid":"1932f287-552d-4539-8153-b2fc204a703b","name":"Thygesen, Lau Caspar","name_org":"Thygesen, Lau C.","orcid":"0000-0001-8375-5211","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 5","corresponding":null,"first":"Jan","key":"wohlfahrtpau","last":"Wohlfahrt","name":"Wohlfahrt, Jan","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Ole","key":"raaschounielsenpau","last":"Raaschou-Nielsen","locid":"9ae8831f-2034-4c09-84bc-01cd649e3b55","name":"Raaschou-Nielsen, Ole","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40632980"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1212/wnl.0000000000213920","issn":["00283878","1526632X"],"issn_org":["00283878"],"issue":"3","journal":"Neurology","paperid":"e213920","publisher":"Lippincott Williams and Wilkins","status":"p","title":"Neurology","volume":"105"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105010409796"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Non-Profit Organisations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-NP","DI-NP-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Non-Profit Organisations","Roskilde University","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-132dcd8c-64cf-4296-9466-6607ce2c1bb8","ruc-16356bb6-9739-4be9-b969-c156e3245da2","sdu-2f5ea317-f8a1-43f0-8556-882590a4bb15"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","RUC Roskilde University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Ambient Outdoor Air Pollution and Risk of Tumors of the Central Nervous System","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-099.71-822543","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Background: Platelet transfusions are frequent in the Intensive Care Unit (ICU), either as prophylaxis against bleeding complications or as treatment for bleeding. The European Society of Intensive Care Medicine guidelines for ICU patients generally recommend not using prophylactic platelet transfusions unless the platelet count falls below 10 × 109 cells/L in non-bleeding patients and make no recommendation for platelet transfusion threshold in non-massively bleeding patients with thrombocytopenia. Therefore, the decision to transfuse platelets is often left to clinical assessment by the treating physician. This study aims to describe current platelet transfusion preferences among ICU physicians. Methods: An online, anonymous survey consisting of 43 items was produced in two languages (French and English) and distributed by investigators in the Nine-I research network to ICU physicians in Europe and the United States of America. The survey evaluated platelet transfusion practices in ICU patients with and without bleeding, the presence of local guidelines, and factors influencing the decisions to transfuse platelets. Only completed surveys were analysed. Results: We received 997 surveys completed by ICU physicians. Overall, there was large heterogeneity in platelet transfusion practices between and within countries. In non-bleeding, thrombocytopenic medical ICU patients, most would transfuse prophylactic platelets at a platelet count threshold of 10 × 109 cells/L. Thirty percent would change their strategy in patients with bone marrow failure and either be more liberal (60%; 95% Confidence Limits 0.54, 0.66), more restrictive (31%; 0.26,0.36) or seek assistance. Higher thresholds were preferred in surgical patients, prior to procedures and in patients with bleeding. Only 173 (17%; 0.15,0.19) responded that they were confident about the clinical indications every time they prescribed a platelet transfusion. As for existing guidelines, only 123 (12%; 0.10,0.15) responded that they always read them. Colleagues' attitudes and departmental culture were important influencers on transfusion practice. Conclusion: Platelet transfusion practice in the ICU is heterogeneous, both between and within countries; guidelines are often not used, and there is often uncertainty about the clinical indication.","author_count":26,"contributors":["Russell, Lene","Azoulay, Elie","Anthon, Carl Thomas","Pène, Frédéric","Akella, Padmastuti","Mabrouki, Asma","Puxty, Kathryn","Nielsen, Lene Bjerregaard","Andreasen, Jo Bønding","Kander, Thomas","Sjövall, Fredrik","Hästbacka, Johanna","Hvas, Christine Lodberg","Van De Louw, Andry","Chawla, Sanjay","Bauer, Philippe R.","Castro, Pedro","Povoa, Pedro","Coelho, Luis","Fernandez, Sara","Topeli, Arzu","Barratt-Due, Andreas","Barbaglio, Caterina","Kochanek, Matthias","Martin-Loeches, Ignacio","Kentish-Barnes, Nancy"],"cuid":["S03-100.00-822655"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["America, North","Asia, Middle East","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Russell, Lene"],"country":["Finland","France","Germany","Ireland","Italy","Norway","Portugal","Spain","Sweden","Turkey","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["AP-HP Assistance Publique - Hopitaux de Paris","All Global Affiliations","Assistance publique – Hôpitaux de Paris","CIBERES - Centro de Investigación Biomédica en Red de Enfermedades Respiratorias","Cornell University","Fundacion Jimenez Dıaz and CIBER de Enfermedades Respiratorias (CIBERES)","Glasgow Royal Infirmary","Hacettepe University","Hospital de Sao Francisco Xavierl","Hospital de São Francisco Xavier","Hôpitaux de Paris","Instituto de Salud Carlos III","Lund University","Mayo Clinic College of Medicine and Science","Mayo College of Medicine","Memorial Sloan-Kettering Cancer Center","NHS Greater Glasgow and Clyde","NOVA University Lisbon","Niguarda Ho