{"abstract":"The 68th Benzon Foundation Symposium brought together leading experts to explore the integration of mass spectrometry–based proteomics and artificial intelligence to revolutionize personalized medicine. This report highlights key discussions on recent technological advances in mass spectrometry–based proteomics, including improvements in sensitivity, throughput, and data analysis. Particular emphasis was placed on plasma proteomics and its potential for biomarker discovery across various diseases. The symposium addressed critical challenges in translating proteomic discoveries to clinical practice, including standardization, regulatory considerations, and the need for robust “business cases” to motivate adoption. Promising applications were presented in areas such as cancer diagnostics, neurodegenerative diseases, and cardiovascular health. The integration of proteomics with other omics technologies and imaging methods was explored, showcasing the power of multimodal approaches in understanding complex biological systems. Artificial intelligence emerged as a crucial tool for the acquisition of large-scale proteomic datasets, extracting meaningful insights, and enhancing clinical decision-making. By fostering dialog between academic researchers, industry leaders in proteomics technology, and clinicians, the symposium illuminated potential pathways for proteomics to transform personalized medicine, advancing the cause of more precise diagnostics and targeted therapies.","author_count":15,"contributors":["Albrecht, Vincent","Müller-Reif, Johannes","Nordmann, Thierry M.","Mund, Andreas","Schweizer, Lisa","Geyer, Philipp E.","Niu, Lili","Wang, Juanjuan","Post, Frederik","Oeller, Marc","Metousis, Andreas","Nielsen, Annelaura Bach","Steger, Medini","Wewer Albrechtsen, Nicolai J","Mann, Matthias"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-811079"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Max Planck Institute of Biochemistry"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"OT":["BioInnovation Institute"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-NOVO"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-PR-NOVO","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"bridging the gap from proteomics technology to clinical application highlights from the 000000000068th benzon foundation symposium","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mcpro.2024.100877","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-811079","identifier":[{"id":"85211015211","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC11652764","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"39522756","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-ce69e58c-17fe-41f3-afe8-56964ccad262"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-CC8C24768A99B45A281F9442916CA5F0"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mcpro.2024.100877","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/446749105/1-s2.0-S1535947624001671-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"max planck institute of biochemistry","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Max Planck Institute of Biochemistry","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Protein Research, Mann Group"],"nora_code":