{"abstract":"Successful oral delivery of liposomes requires formulations designed to withstand harsh gastrointestinal conditions, e.g., by converting to solid-state followed by loading into gastro-resistant delivery devices. The hypothesis was that the use of dextran-trehalose mixtures for spray drying would improve the rehydration kinetics of dried liposomes. The objectives were to determine the protective capacity of trehalose-dextran dehydration precursors and to increase the concentration of liposomes in the dry formulation volume. The study successfully demonstrated that 8.5% dextran combined with 76.5% trehalose protected CAF®04 liposomes during drying, with the liposome content maintained at 15% of the dry powder. Accordingly, the rehydration kinetics were slightly improved in formulations containing up to 8.5% dextran in the dry powder volume. Additionally, a 2.4-fold increase in lipid concentration (3 mM vs 7.245 mM) was achieved for spray dried CAF®04 liposomes. Ultimately, this study demonstrates the significance of trehalose as a primary carrier during spray drying of CAF®04 liposomes and highlights the advantage of incorporating small amounts of dextran to tune rehydration kinetics of spray-dried liposomes.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Lutta, Anitta Awuor","Knopp, Matthias M.","Tollemeto, Matteo","Pedersen, Gabriel K.","Schmidt, Signe T.","Grohganz, Holger","Nielsen, Line Hagner"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-724988"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Lutta, Anitta Awuor"],"keyword":["Dextran","Liposomes","Rehydration kinetics","Solid solubility","Spray drying","Trehalose"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GTS","DI-GV","DI-U"],"level3":{"GTS":["DI-GTS-ALL","DI-GTS-BION"],"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GTS-BION","DI-GV-SSI","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the interplay between trehalose and dextran as spray drying precursors for cationic liposomes","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijpharm.2024.123798","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-724988","identifier":[{"id":"38190949","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85184752254","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-08ac19fd-439c-46e3-a917-79ba42034261","ku-eb8b44ab-90d0-49ce-b01b-ef6221a53642"],"keyword":["Dextran","Liposomes","Rehydration kinetics","Solid solubility","Spray drying","Trehalose"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-C8D86456E806A0DD3CA3FB89C02CC4B6"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/383088102/1_s2.0_S0378517324000322_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijpharm.2024.123798","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/353611505/1-s2.0-S0378517324000322-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Health Technology","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Health Technology, Drug Delivery and Sensing","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Health Technology, Drug Delivery and Sensing, Pharmaceutical technology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3