"2023"}
{"abstract":"In the mammalian DNA damage response, ADP-ribosylation signalling is of crucial importance to mark sites of DNA damage as well as recruit and regulate repairs factors. Specifically, the PARP1:HPF1 complex recognises damaged DNA and catalyses the formation of serine-linked ADP-ribosylation marks (mono-Ser-ADPr), which are extended into ADP-ribose polymers (poly-Ser-ADPr) by PARP1 alone. Poly-Ser-ADPr is reversed by PARG, while the terminal mono-Ser-ADPr is removed by ARH3. Despite its significance and apparent evolutionary conservation, little is known about ADP-ribosylation signalling in non-mammalian Animalia. The presence of HPF1, but absence of ARH3, in some insect genomes, including Drosophila species, raises questions regarding the existence and reversal of serine-ADP-ribosylation in these species. Here we show by quantitative proteomics that Ser-ADPr is the major form of ADP-ribosylation in the DNA damage response of Drosophila melanogaster and is dependent on the dParp1:dHpf1 complex. Moreover, our structural and biochemical investigations uncover the mechanism of mono-Ser-ADPr removal by Drosophila Parg. Collectively, our data reveal PARP:HPF1-mediated Ser-ADPr as a defining feature of the DDR in Animalia. The striking conservation within this kingdom suggests that organisms that carry only a core set of ADP-ribosyl metabolising enzymes, such as Drosophila, are valuable model organisms to study the physiological role of Ser-ADPr signalling.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Fontana, Pietro","Buch-Larsen, Sara C.","Suyari, Osamu","Smith, Rebecca","Suskiewicz, Marcin J.","Schützenhofer, Kira","Ariza, Antonio","Rack, Johannes Gregor Matthias","Nielsen, Michael L.","Ahel, Ivan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-678537"],"filters":{"added":"2023-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["France","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Boston Children's Hospital","Centre National de la Recherche Scientifique (CNRS)","Harvard University","Sir William Dunn School of Pathology","University of Exeter","University of Sheffield"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"serine adp ribosylation in drosophila provides insights into the evolution of reversible adp ribosylation signalling","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-38793-y","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-678537","identifier":[{"id":"37268618","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85160897386","type":"scopus"},{"id":"001003996200015","type":"wos"},{"id":"PMC10238386","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-815c84e3-8978-486e-9ee5-dea292f6cbfb"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-2C89128BF5BE361BA59CBFA6EBFBB7EF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-38793-y","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/357615675/s41467_023_38793_y.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"sir william dunn school of pathology","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Sir William Dunn School of Pathology","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"harvard university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Harvard University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"boston children s hospital","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Boston Children's Hospital","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Protein Research, Nielsen Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"fr","key":"centre national de la recherche scientifique","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Centre National de la Recherche Scientifique (CNRS)","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"gb","key":"university of sheffield","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Sheffield","used":1},{"affno":"7","country":"gb","key":"university of exeter","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Exeter","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2 3","first":"Pietro","key":"fontanapau","last":"Fontana","name":"Fontana, Pietro","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Sara C.","key":"buchlarsenpau","last":"Buch-Larsen","locid":"975fd164-f221-4968-a0d4-1e50e7887842","name":"Buch-Larsen, Sara C.","orcid":"0000-0001-6250-5467","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Osamu","key":"suyaripau","last":"Suyari","name":"Suyari, Osamu","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Rebecca","key":"smithpau","last":"Smith","name":"Smith, Rebecca","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 5","first":"Marcin J.","key":"suskiewiczpau","last":"Suskiewicz","name":"Suskiewicz, Marcin J.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Kira","key":"schützenhoferpau","last":"Schützenhofer","name":"Schützenhofer, Kira","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 6","first":"Antonio","key":"arizapau","last":"Ariza","name":"Ariza, Antonio","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 7","first":"Johannes Gregor Matthias","key":"rackpau","last":"Rack","name":"Rack, Johannes Gregor Matthias","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Michael L.","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"b72d98f7-79fa-43a7-8066-783ed8034146","name":"Nielsen, Michael L.","orcid":"0000-0002-0067-9039","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Ivan","key":"ahelpau","last":"Ahel","name":"Ahel, Ivan","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37268618"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41467-023-38793-y","issn":["20411723"],"issue":"1","journal":"Nature Communications","paperid":"3200","status":"p","title":"Nature Communications","volume":"14"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85160897386"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-815c84e3-8978-486e-9ee5-dea292f6cbfb"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Serine ADP-ribosylation in Drosophila provides insights into the evolution of reversible ADP-ribosylation signalling","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-678537","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Allogeneic transplantation (allo-HCT) is a curative treatment in CLL whose efficacy including the most severe forms had led to the 2006 EBMT recommendations. The advent after 2014 of targeted therapies has revolutionized CLL management, allowing prolonged control to patients who have failed immunochemotherapy and/or have TP53 alterations. We analysed the pre COVID pandemic 2009–2019 EBMT registry. The yearly number of allo-HCT raised to 458 in 2011 yet dropped from 2013 onwards to an apparent plateau above 100. Within the 10 countries who were under the EMA for drug approval and performed 83.5% of those procedures, large initial differences were found but the annual number converged to 2–3 per 10 million inhabitants during the 3 most recent years suggesting that allo-HCT remains applied in selected patients. Long-term follow-up on targeted therapies shows that most patients relapse, some early, with risk factors and resistance mechanisms being described. The treatment of patients exposed to both BCL2 and BTK inhibitors and especially those with double refractory disease will become a challenge in which allo-HCT remains a solid option in competition with emerging therapies that have yet to demonstrate their long-term effectiveness.","author_count":32,"contributors":["Tournilhac, Olivier","van Gelder, Michel","Eikema, Dirk Jan","Zinger, Nienke","Dreger, Peter","Bornhäuser, Martin","Vucinic, Vladan","Scheid, Christof","Cornelissen, Jan J.","Schroeder, Thomas","Jindra, Pavel","Sengeløv, Henrik","Nguyen Quoc, Stephanie","Stelljes, Matthias","Blau, Igor Wolfgang","Mayer, Jiri","Paneesha, Shankara","Chevallier, Patrice","Forcade, Edouard","Kröger, Nicolaus","Blaise, Didier","Gribben, John","Nielsen, Bendt","Johansson, Jan Erik","Kyriakou, Charalampia","Beguin, Yves","Pioltelli, Pietro","Sampol, Antònia","McLornan, Donal P.","Schetelig, Johannes","Hayden, Patrick J.","Yakoub-Agha, Ibrahim"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-748624"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Gl