{"abstract":"Background: Air pollution is a growing concern worldwide, with significant impacts on human health. Multiple myeloma is a type of blood cancer with increasing incidence. Studies have linked air pollution exposure to various types of cancer, including leukemia and lymphoma, however, the relationship with multiple myeloma incidence has not been extensively investigated. Methods: We pooled four European cohorts (N = 234,803) and assessed the association between residential exposure to nitrogen dioxide (NO2), fine particles (PM2.5), black carbon (BC), and ozone (O3) and multiple myeloma. We applied Cox proportional hazards models adjusting for potential confounders at the individual and area-level. Results: During 4,415,817 person-years of follow-up (average 18.8 years), we observed 404 cases of multiple myeloma. The results of the fully adjusted linear analyses showed hazard ratios (95% confidence interval) of 0.99 (0.84, 1.16) per 10 μg/m³ NO2, 1.04 (0.82, 1.33) per 5 μg/m³ PM2.5, 0.99 (0.84, 1.18) per 0.5 10−5 m−1 BCE, and 1.11 (0.87, 1.41) per 10 μg/m³ O3. Conclusions: We did not observe an association between long-term ambient air pollution exposure and incidence of multiple myeloma.","author_count":29,"contributors":["Hvidtfeldt, Ulla Arthur","Chen, Jie","Rodopoulou, Sophia","Strak, Maciej","de Hoogh, Kees","Andersen, Zorana J.","Bellander, Tom","Brandt, Jørgen","Forastiere, Francesco","Brynedal, Boel","Hertel, Ole","Hoffmann, Barbara","Katsouyanni, Klea","Ketzel, Matthias","Leander, Karin","Magnusson, Patrik K.E.","Nagel, Gabriele","Pershagen, Göran","Rizzuto, Debora","Samoli, Evangelia","So, Rina","Stafoggia, Massimo","Tjønneland, Anne","Weinmayr, Gudrun","Wolf, Kathrin","Zitt, Emanuel","Brunekreef, Bert","Hoek, Gerard","Raaschou-Nielsen, Ole"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-716121"],"filters":{"added":"2023-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Hvidtfeldt, Ulla Arthur"],"country":["Austria","Germany","Greece","Italy","Netherlands","Sweden","Switzerland","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["Agency for Preventive and Social Medicine","All Global Affiliations","Dept Epidemiol Lazio Reg","Heinrich Heine University Düsseldorf","Helmholtz Association of German Research Centres","Helmholtz Zentrum München - German Research Center for Environmental Health","Imperial College London","Karolinska Institutet","LKH Feldkirch","National Institute for Public Health and the Environment (RIVM)","National and Kapodistrian University of Athens","Stockholm University","Swiss Tropical and Public Health Institute","Ulm University","University College London","University Medical Centre Utrecht","University of Athens","University of Basel","University of Surrey","Utrecht University","aks gesundheit GmbH"],"keyword":["Air Pollutants toxicity","Air Pollution adverse effects","Air pollution","Cohort Studies","Environmental Exposure adverse effects","Humans","Multiple myeloma","Nitrogen Dioxide toxicity","Nitrogen dioxide","Ozone","Particulate Matter analysis","Particulate matter"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-NP","DI-U"],"level3":{"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-CAN"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-NP-CAN","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"multiple myeloma risk in relation to long term air pollution exposure a pooled analysis of four european cohorts","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85173259485","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-716121","identifier":[{"id":"85173259485","type":"scopus"},{"id":"37806476","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-d8325f63-ee8e-4519-9b40-83907d3b7d97","ku-cea385a6-4bea-4a08-bb75-68963d14e983"],"keyword":["Air Pollutants/