":"wrightpau","last":"Wright","name":"Wright, Christopher John","rank":24,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lene","key":"russellpau","last":"Russell","name":"Russell, Lene","rank":25,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37812225"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00134-023-07225-2","issn":["03424642","09351701"],"issn_org":["03424642"],"issue":"11","journal":"Intensive Care Medicine","pages":"1327-1338","publisher":"Springer Science+Business Media","status":"p","title":"Intensive Care Medicine","volume":"49"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85173919249"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","North Denmark Region","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-3fad97e7-4f48-4344-ae4b-da65d16ce35e"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Thrombocytopenia and platelet transfusions in ICU patients : an international inception cohort study (PLOT-ICU)","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-713884","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Background: Air pollution is a well-recognized risk factor for cardiovascular disease. However, the mechanistic pathways underlying the association are not completely understood. Hence, further studies are required to shed light on potential mechanisms, through which air pollution may affect the development from subclinical to clinical cardiovascular disease. Objectives: To investigate associations between short-term exposure to air pollution and high-density lipoprotein (HDL), non-high density lipoprotein (non-HDL), systolic and diastolic blood pressure. Methods: The study was conducted among 32,851 Danes from the Diet, Cancer and Health – Next Generations cohort, who had a blood sample taken and blood pressure measured. We measured HDL and non-HDL in the blood samples. We modelled exposure to fine particulate matter (PM 2.5), ultrafine particles (UFP), elemental carbon (EC) and nitrogen dioxide (NO 2) in time-windows from 24 h up to 90 days before blood sampling. Pollutants were modelled as total air pollution from all sources, and apportioned into contributions from non-traffic and traffic sources. We analyzed data using linear and logistic regression, with adjustment for socio-economic and lifestyle factors. Results: Air pollution exposure over 24 h to 30 days was generally adversely associated with lipid profile and blood pressure, e.g. for 30-day UFP-exposure, adjusted β-estimates were: −0.025 (−0.043; −0.006) for HDL, 0.086 (0.042; 0.130) for non-HDL, 2.45 (1.70; 3.11) for systolic and 1.56 (1.07; 20.4) for diastolic blood pressure, per 10,000 particles/cm 3. The strongest associations were found for the non-traffic components of air pollution, and among those who were overweight/obese. Discussion: In this large study of air pollution and lipid levels and blood pressure, we found that 24-h to 30-day PM 2.5, UFP, EC and NO 2 concentrations were generally adversely associated with lipid profile and blood pressure, two important cardiovascular risk factors. The study suggests potential pathways, through which air pollution could affect the development of cardiovascular disease.","author_count":14,"contributors":["Roswall, Nina","Poulsen, Aslak Harbo","Arthur Hvidtfeldt, Ulla","Hendriksen, Peter Fjeldstad","Boll, Katja","Halkjær, Jytte","Ketzel, Matthias","Brandt, Jørgen","Frohn, Lise Marie","Christensen, Jesper Heile","Im, Ulas","Sørensen, Mette","Raaschou-Nielsen, Ole"],"cuid":["S02-098.97-018762"],"filters":{"added":"2023-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Roswall, Nina"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Surrey"],"keyword":["Blood Pressure","Elemental carbon","Lipid profile","Nitrogen dioxide","PM2 5","Ultra fine particles"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-NP","DI-U"],"level3":{"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-CAN"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-NP-CAN","DI-U-AU","DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"exposure to ambient air pollution and lipid levels and blood pressure in an adult danish cohort","src":["au","ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-RUC"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85145857351","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-098.97-018762","identifier":[{"id":"36584852","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85145857351","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-536b4675-1c85-422b-b9be-c6bd262456b3","ruc-d0c026fb-0ced-4748-b5e6-3f4acaf77917"],"keyword":["Blood pressure","Elemental carbon","Lipid profile","Nitrogen dioxide","PM2.5","Ultra-fine particles"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-711E26B4DA56AC2E9E2D124D76C7232A"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"dcrc danish cancer society research center","l2":"