,"volume":"178"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85165505340"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Non-Profit Organisations","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-NP","DI-NP-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Non-Profit Organisations","Other Research","Roskilde University","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-4c8f7ba3-ea79-4fb4-9380-96df94973edb","ku-c8358cd2-6199-4e34-ab4e-b09e3b1f25ca","ruc-79c3914e-1046-4696-a865-032691c456c3"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","RUC Roskilde University"],"title":"Exposure to long-term source-specific transportation noise and incident breast cancer : A pooled study of eight Nordic cohorts","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-099.90-681366","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Much of the biopharmaceutical industry’s success over the past 30 years has relied on products derived from Chinese Hamster Ovary (CHO) cell lines. During this time, improvements in mammalian cell cultures have come from cell line development and process optimization suited for large-scale fed-batch processes. Originally developed for high cell densities and sensitive products, perfusion processes have a long history. Driven by high volumetric titers and a small footprint, perfusion-based bioprocess research has regained an interest from academia and industry. The recent pandemic has further highlighted the need for such intensified biomanufacturing options. In this review, we outline the technical history of research in this field as it applies to biologics production in CHO cells. We demonstrate a number of emerging trends in the literature and corroborate these with underlying drivers in the commercial space. From these trends, we speculate that the future of perfusion bioprocesses is bright and that the fields of media optimization, continuous processing, and cell line engineering hold the greatest potential. Aligning in its continuous setup with the demands for Industry 4.0, perfusion biomanufacturing is likely to be a hot topic in the years to come.","author_count":13,"contributors":["MacDonald, Michael A.","Nöbel, Matthias","Roche Recinos, Dinora","Martínez, Verónica S.","Schulz, Benjamin L.","Howard, Christopher B.","Baker, Kym","Shave, Evan","Lee, Yih Yean","Marcellin, Esteban","Mahler, Stephen","Nielsen, Lars K.","Munro, Trent"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-335623"],"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD","Oceania"],"corresponding_author":["Munro, Trent"],"country":["Australia","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","CSL Limited","Thermo Fisher Scientific, Inc.","University of Queensland"],"keyword":["Biopharmaceuticals","Bioprocessing","Bioreactor","Cell line development","Chinese hamster ovary cells","Genetic engineering","Industry 4 0","Perfusion","Process optimization"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"perfusion culture of chinese hamster ovary cells for bioprocessing applications","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"djr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85120164675","version":"Other Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["The authors thank the National Biologics Facility, the Australian Research Council Training Centre for Biopharmaceutical Innovation, the Australian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology and the University of Queensland. L. K. N. was supported by Novo Nordisk Foundation grants NNF10CC1016517 and NNF14OC0009473."],"id":"S01-000.00-335623","identifier":[{"id":"34844499","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85120164675","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-67f5a323-1367-4520-928b-e7b232c33cee"],"keyword":["Chinese Hamster Ovary cells","Industry 4.0","Perfusion","biopharmaceuticals","bioprocessing","bioreactor","cell line development","genetic engineering","process optimization"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-180E8A750E7C5A23C2B14E73AD4BF747"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"au","key":"university of queensland","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"University of Queensland","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"thermo fisher scientific inc","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Thermo Fisher Scientific, Inc.","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"au","key":"csl limited","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"CSL Limited","used":1},{"affno":"