pub":{"doi":"10.1136/gutjnl-2021-325753","issn":["00175749","14683288"],"issn_org":["00175749"],"issue":"12","journal":"Gut","pages":"2463-2480","publisher":"BMJ Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"Gut","volume":"71"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85125251896"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-219f39a3-6a2d-4a45-931c-21765f684d97","sdu-f7fa38d6-d272-4df5-9f4c-ade30ed0eab0"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Impairment of gut microbial biotin metabolism and host biotin status in severe obesity : effect of biotin and prebiotic supplementation on improved metabolism","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-095.80-040793","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Background: Dioxins and polychlorobiphenyls (PCBs) are persistent organic pollutants that have demonstrated endocrine disrupting properties. Several of these chemicals are carcinogenic and positive associations have been suggested with breast cancer risk. In general population, diet represents the main source of exposure. Methods: Associations between dietary intake of 17 dioxins and 35 PCBs and breast cancer were evaluated in the European Prospective Investigation into Cancer and Nutrition (EPIC) cohort from nine European countries using multivariable Cox regressions. The present study included 318,607 women (mean ± SD age: 50.7 ± 9.7) with 13,241 incident invasive breast cancers and a median follow-up of 14.9 years (IQR = 13.5–16.4). Dietary intake of dioxins and PCBs was assessed combining EPIC food consumption data with food contamination data provided by the European Food Safety Authority. Results: Exposure to dioxins, dioxins + Dioxin-Like-PCBs, Dioxin-Like-PCBs (DL-PCBs), and Non-Dioxin-Like-PCBs (NDL-PCBs) estimated from reported dietary intakes were not associated with breast cancer incidence, with the following hazard ratios (HRs) and 95% confidence intervals for an increment of 1 SD: HRdioxins = 1.00 (0.98 to 1.02), HRdioxins+DL-PCB = 1.01 (0.98 to 1.03), HRDL-PCB = 1.01 (0.98 to 1.03), and HRNDL-PCB = 1.01 (0.99 to 1.03). Results remained unchanged when analyzing intakes as quintile groups, as well as when analyses were run separately per country, or separating breast cancer cases based on estrogen receptor status or after further adjustments on main contributing food groups to PCBs and dioxins intake and nutritional factors. Conclusions: This large European prospective study does not support the hypothesis of an association between dietary intake of dioxins and PCBs and breast cancer risk.","author_count":30,"contributors":["Fiolet, Thibault","Casagrande, Corinne","Nicolas, Geneviève","Horvath, Zsuzsanna","Frenoy, Pauline","Weiderpass, Elisabete","Katzke, Verena","Kaaks, Rudolf","Rodriguez-Barranco, Miguel","Panico, Salvatore","Sacerdote, Carlotta","Manjer, Jonas","Sonestedt, Emily","Grioni, Sara","Agudo, Antonio","Rylander, Charlotta","Haugdahl Nøst, Therese","Skeie, Guri","Tjønneland, Anne","Raaschou-Nielsen, Ole","Ardanaz, Eva","Amiano, Pilar","Dolores Chirlaque López, María","Schulze, Matthias B.","Wennberg, Maria","Harlid, Sophia","Cairat, Manon","Kvaskoff, Marina","Huybrechts, Inge","Romana Mancini, Francesca"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-012762"],"filters":{"added":"2022-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Romana Mancini, Francesca"],"country":["France","Germany","Italy","Norway","Spain","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Andalusian School of public health","Azienda Ospedaliera - Universitaria Città della Salute e della Scienza di Torino","Carlos III Health Institute (ISCIII) (CIBER)","Catalan Institute of Oncology-ICO","Escuela Andaluza de Salud Pública (EASP)","European Food Safety Authority","Fondazione IRCCS Istituto Nazionale dei Tumori","German Cancer Research Center","German Cancer Research Center (DKFZ)","German Institute of Human Nutrition Potsdam-Rehbruecke","IRCCS Fondazione Istituto Nazionale per lo studio e la cura dei tumori - Milano","IdiSNA, Navarra Institute for Health Research, Pamplona, Spain.","Institut National de la Santé et de la Recherche Médicale (INSERM)","Instituto de Investigación Biosanitaria ibs","Instituto de Investigación Sanitaria","Instituto de Investigación Sanitaria Biodonostia","International Agency for Research on Cancer","International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC)","Lund University","Nutrition and Metabolism Branch","Paris-Saclay University","Public University of Navarra","Skåne University Hospital","UiT The Arctic University of Norway","Umeå University","Universite Paris-Saclay","University of Murcia","University of Naples Federico II","University of Potsdam","University of Tromsø – The Arctic University of Norway","University of Turin"],"keyword":["Adult","Breast Neoplasms epidemiology","Breast cancer","Diet","Dioxins","Dioxins adverse effects","Eating","Female","Food Contamination analysis","Humans","Male","Middle Aged","PCBs","Persistent pollutants","Polychlorinated Biphenyls adverse effects","Prospective Studies"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-NP","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-CAN"],"OT":["Navarra Institute for Health Research, Pamplona, Navarra, Spain."],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-NP-CAN","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"dietary intakes of dioxins and polychlorobiphenyls pcbs and breast cancer risk in 000000000009 european countries","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envint.2022.107213","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-012762","identifier":[{"id":"35364416","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85127167792","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-025f1db3-69ed-464e-92f9-1c159455099e","ku-98bdf9ab-ae63-44f6-92da-d04eed5c25d3"],"keyword":["Adult","Breast Neoplasms/epidemiology","Breast cancer","Diet","Dioxins","Dioxins/adverse effects","Eating","Female","Food Contamination/analysis","Humans","Male","Middle Aged","PCBs","Persistent pollutants","Polychlorinated Biphenyls/adverse effects","Polychlorobiphenyls","Prospective Studies"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-AB3DB63AFA2DF0882AEB76D851B22F4B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/307733181/Dietary.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type