{"abstract":"Air pollutants such as NO 2 and PM 2.5 have consistently been linked to mortality, but only few previous studies have addressed associations with long-term exposure to black carbon (BC) and ozone (O 3). We investigated the association between PM 2.5, PM 10, BC, NO 2, and O 3 and mortality in a Danish cohort of 49,564 individuals who were followed up from enrollment in 1993–1997 through 2015. Residential address history from 1979 onwards was combined with air pollution exposure obtained by the state-of-the-art, validated, THOR/AirGIS air pollution modelling system, and information on residential traffic noise exposure, lifestyle and socio-demography. We observed higher risks of all-cause as well as cardiovascular disease (CVD) mortality with higher long-term exposure to PM 2.5, PM 10, BC, and NO 2. For PM 2.5 and CVD mortality, a hazard ratio (HR) of 1.29 (95% CI: 1.13–1.47) per 5 μg/m 3 was observed, and correspondingly HRs of 1.16 (95% CI: 1.05–1.27) and 1.11 (95% CI: 1.04–1.17) were observed for BC (per 1 μg/m 3) and NO 2 (per 10 μg/m 3), respectively. Adjustment for noise gave slightly lower estimates for the air pollutants and CVD mortality. Inverse relationships were observed for O 3. None of the investigated air pollutants were related to risk of respiratory mortality. Stratified analyses suggested that the elevated risks of CVD and all-cause mortality in relation to long-term PM, NO 2 and BC exposure were restricted to males. This study supports a role of PM, BC, and NO 2 in all-cause and CVD mortality independent of road traffic noise exposure.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Hvidtfeldt, Ulla Arthur","Sørensen, Mette","Geels, Camilla","Ketzel, Matthias","Khan, Jibran","Tjønneland, Anne","Overvad, Kim","Brandt, Jørgen","Raaschou-Nielsen, Ole"],"cuid":["S03-098.60-018289"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["Asia, Middle East","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Qatar","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Qatar University","University of Surrey"],"keyword":["Black carbon","CVD","Epidemiology","Mortality","NO2","Ozone","Particulate matter"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-NP","DI-U"],"level3":{"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-CAN"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-NP-CAN","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"long term residential exposure to pm000000000002 000000000005 pm000000000010 black carbon no000000000002 and ozone and mortality in a danish cohort","src":["au","ku","ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envint.2018.12.010","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S03-098.60-018289","identifier":[{"id":"000455532500030","type":"wos"},{"id":"85058142004","type":"scopus"},{"id":"30551059","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-4d1c6df1-6a0a-46ce-bf4f-576e54034a05","ku-0975310a-1ed8-4f19-b2bc-6b2ca2f2ab50","ruc-146f8799-a614-4a25-8aac-19380f4ac839"],"keyword":["Black carbon","CVD","Epidemiology","Mortality","NO","NO2","Ozone","Particulate matter"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-879FD32C3472FD62DFC75CB755A3E711"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envint.2018.12.010","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://rucforsk.ruc.dk/ws/files/67676780/1_s2.0_S016041201831969X_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/212454310/Long_term_residential_exposure_to_PM2.5_PM10_black_carbon_NO2_and_ozone_and_mortality_in_a_Danish_cohort.pdf","version":"Published V