{"abstract":"Current clinical ultrasound (US) systems are limited to show blood flow movement in either 1-D or 2-D. In this paper, a method for estimating 3-D vector velocities in a plane using the transverse oscillation method, a 32×32 element matrix array, and the experimental US scanner SARUS is presented. The aim of this paper is to estimate precise flow rates and peak velocities derived from 3-D vector flow estimates. The emission sequence provides 3-D vector flow estimates at up to 1.145 frames/s in a plane, and was used to estimate 3-D vector flow in a cross-sectional image plane. The method is validated in two phantom studies, where flow rates are measured in a flow-rig, providing a constant parabolic flow, and in a straight-vessel phantom ( ø=8 mm) connected to a flow pump capable of generating time varying waveforms. Flow rates are estimated to be 82.1 ± 2.8 L/min in the flow-rig compared with the expected 79.8 L/min, and to 2.68 ± 0.04 mL/stroke in the pulsating environment compared with the expected 2.57 ± 0.08 mL/stroke. Flow rates estimated in the common carotid artery of a healthy volunteer are compared with magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) measured flow rates using a 1-D through-plane velocity sequence. Mean flow rates were 333 ± 31 mL/min for the presented method and 346 ± 2 mL/min for the MRI measurements.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Holbek, Simon","Ewertsen, Caroline","Bouzari, Hamed","Pihl, Michael Johannes","Hansen, Kristoffer Lindskov","Stuart, Matthias Bo","Thomsen, Carsten","Nielsen, Michael Bachmann","Jensen, Jørgen Arendt"],"cuid":["S02-094.79-033429"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["3 D vector flow imaging","Apertures","Blood","Blood flow","Estimation","MRI","Magnetic Resonance Imaging","Medical ultrasound","Transducers","Transverse oscillation","Ultrasonic imaging"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"ultrasonic 000000000003 d vector flow method for quantitative i in vivo i peak velocity and flow rate estimation","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/190320031/TUFFC2639318.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S02-094.79-033429","identifier":[{"id":"27992335","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85015234602","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-25cafcb6-c4db-4f57-94f8-8658fa5edaf6","ku-15b8eba7-ceb3-4b02-b6a5-de7c7ac25872"],"keyword":["3-D vector flow imaging","Apertures","Blood","Blood flow","Estimation","MRI","Magnetic resonance imaging","Transducers","Ultrasonic imaging","medical ultrasound","transverse oscillation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-0FFC44C9EA5FEC4EF2DA2F84223FC8F2"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/190320031/TUFFC2639318.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering, Biomedical Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhage