lecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"first":"Kim","key":"overvadkimpau","last":"Overvad","locid":"83738c57-45d4-41e0-b807-ad0f6dec597c","name":"Overvad, Kim","orcid":"0000-0001-6429-7921","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Ole","key":"raaschounielsenolepau","last":"Raaschou-Nielsen","name":"Raaschou-Nielsen, Ole","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ole Lundsgaard","key":"raaschounielsenolelundsgaardpau","last":"Raaschou-Nielsen","name":"Raaschou-Nielsen, Ole Lundsgaard","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["22052517"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1161/strokeaha.111.629246","issn":["00392499","15244628"],"issn_org":["00392499"],"issue":"2","journal":"Stroke","pages":"320-325","publisher":"Lippincott Williams & Wilkins","status":"p","title":"Stroke","volume":"43"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84856490130"],"sdg":["3. Good Health and Well-being","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Non-Profit Organisations","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-NP","DI-NP-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Non-Profit Organisations","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-fa131ac8-7f57-45a9-8b2d-5d91f04fae15","dtu-aa198a39-84f9-487d-9c30-f4f0635b50cb","ku-ce0ca078-54dd-4ea0-b562-7750f9201dc7"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Stroke and Long-Term Exposure to Outdoor Air Pollution From Nitrogen Dioxide: A Cohort Study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-095.91-030663","year":"2012","yearsub":"2012"}
{"abstract":"Background: Short-term exposure to air pollution has been associated with changes in blood pressure (BP) and emergency department visits for hypertension, but little is known about the effects of long-term exposure to traffic-related air pollution on BP and hypertension.Objectives: We studied whether long-term exposure to air pollution is associated with BP and hypertension.Methods: In 1993-1997, 57,053 participants 50-64 years of age were enrolled in a population-based cohort study. Systolic and diastolic BP (SBP and DBP, respectively) were measured at enrollment. Self-reported incident hypertension during a mean follow-up of 5.3 years was assessed by questionnaire. We used a validated dispersion model to estimate residential long-term nitrogen oxides (NOx), a marker of traffic-related air pollution, for the 1- and 5-year periods prior to enrollment and before a diagnosis of hypertension. We conducted a cross-sectional analysis of associations between air pollution and BP at enrollment with linear regression, adjusting for traffic noise, measured short-term NOx, temperature, relative humidity, and potential lifestyle confounders (n = 44,436). We analyzed incident hypertension with Cox regression, adjusting for traffic noise and potential confounders.Results: A doubling of NOx exposure during 1- and 5-year periods preceding enrollment was associated with 0.53-mmHg decreases [95% confidence interval (CI): -0.88, -0.19 mmHg] and 0.50-mmHg decreases (95% CI: -0.84, -0.16 mmHg) in SBP, respectively. Long-term exposure also was associated with a lower prevalence of baseline self-reported hypertension (per doubling of 5-year mean NOx: odds ratio = 0.96; 95% CI: 0.91, 1.00), whereas long-term NOx exposure was not associated with incident self-reported hypertension during follow-up.Conclusions: Long-term exposure to traffic-related air pollution was associated with a slightly lower prevalence of BP at baseline, but was not associated with incident hypertension.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Sørensen, Mette","Hoffmann, Barbara","Hvidberg, Martin","Ketzel, Matthias","Jensen, Steen Solvang","Andersen, Zorana Jovanovic","Tjønneland, Anne","Overvad, Kim","Raaschou-Nielsen, Ole Lundsgaard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-236035"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"long term exposure to traffic related air pollution associated with blood pressure and self reported hypertension in a danish cohort","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1289/ehp.1103631","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-236035","identifier":[{"id":"PMC3295339","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"22214647","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84857724225","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-bad959ab-636b-4c86-a92f-1cb396b84b1b"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-170BC344C540AA49A7671A3EE6EB247B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1289/ehp.1103631","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, Department of Environmental Science, Department of Environmental Science - Atmospheric Emissions & Modelling"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Unspecified organisation"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Mette","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","name":"Sørensen, Mette","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Barbara","key":"hoffmannpau","last":"Hoffmann","name":"Hoffmann, Barbara","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin","key":"hvidbergpau","last":"Hvidberg","locid":"caad0d45-bf3a-4a5f-ae85-b78a367b5186","name":"Hvidberg, Martin","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Matthias","key":"ketzelpau","last":"Ketzel","locid":"798900d2-1d54-4c53-a4ca-671c12aa3015","name":"Ketzel, Matthias","orcid":"0000-0001-9519-1935","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Steen Solvang","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"0d52f91a-4eaa-46f9-ab5f-d7fd3c31440d","name":"Jensen, Steen Solvang","orcid":"0000-0001-5923-8441","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Zorana Jovanovic","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","name":"Andersen, Zorana Jovanovic","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Anne","key":"tjønnelandpau","last":"Tjønneland","name":"Tjønneland, Anne","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kim","key":"overvadpau","last":"Overvad","locid":"83738c57-45d4-41e0-b807-ad0f6dec597c","name":"Overvad, Kim","orcid":"0000-0001-6429-7921","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ole Lundsgaard","key":"raaschounielsenpau","last":"Raaschou-Nielsen","name":"Raaschou-Nielsen, Ole Lundsgaard","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["22214647"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1289/ehp.1103631","issn":["00916765","15529924"],"issn_org":["00916765"],"issue":"3","journal":"Environmental Health Perspectives","pages":"418-24","publisher":"Public Health Services, US Dept of Health and Human Services","status":"p","title":"Environmental Health Perspectives","volume":"120"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84857724225"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-bad959ab-636b-4c86-a92f-1cb396b84b1b"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Long-term exposure to traffic-related air pollution associated with blood pressure and self-reported hypertension in a danish cohort","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-236035","year":"2012","yearsub":"2012"}
{"abstract":"The rapid disruption of tropical forests probably imperils global biodiversity more than any other contemporary phenomenon. With deforestation advancing quickly, protected areas are increasingly becoming final refuges for threatened species and natural ecosystem processes. However, many protected areas in the tropics are themselves vulnerable to human encroachment and other environmental stresses. As pressures mount, it is vital to know whether existing reserves can sustain their biodiversity. A critical constraint in addressing this question has been that data describing a broad array of biodiversity groups have been unavailable for a sufficiently large and representative sample of reserves. Here we present a uniquely comprehensive data set on changes over the past 20 to 30 years in 31 functional groups of species and 21 potential drivers of environmental change, for 60 protected areas stratified across the world's major tropical regions. Our analysis reveals great variation in reserve 'health': about half of all reserves have been effective or performed passably, but the rest are experiencing an erosion of biodiversity that is often alarmingly widespread taxonomically and functionally. Habitat disruption, hunting and forest-product exploitation were the strongest predictors of declining reserve health. Crucially, environmental changes immediately outside reserves seemed nearly as important as those inside in determining their ecological fate, with changes inside reserves strongly mirroring those occurring around them. These findings suggest that tropical protected areas are often intimately linked ecologically to their surrounding habitats, and that a failure to stem broad-scale loss and degradation of such habitats could sharply increase the likelihood of serious biodiversity declines.","author_count":218,"contributors":["Laurance, William F","Carolina Useche, D","Rendeiro, Julio","Kalka, Margareta","Bradshaw, Corey J A","Sloan, Sean P","Laurance, Su